Save this program as `hello.hy`:

```csharp
public class Program {
    public static int Main(string[] args) {
        string name = "World";
        if (args.Length > 0) { name = args[0]; }
        System.Console.WriteLine("Hello, " + name);
        return 0;
    }
}
```

```bash
hy compile hello.hy -o hello
chmod +x hello
./hello Hydrogen
```

For repeat runs, execute `./hello` directly. The release compiler produces native
executables; it does not have an interpreted `run` command.

## Entry point and arguments

`Main` must be a static class method returning `int` or `void`. It accepts no
parameters or one `string[]` parameter. Arguments exclude the executable name;
an integer result becomes the process exit status.

Array access is checked, so test `args.Length` before indexing. UEFI output is
different: an EFI application is booted by firmware, not run from Linux.

Hydrogen `alpha-0.0.2` can produce a UEFI boot application and a constrained
early-kernel image. Both are PE32+ EFI applications for x86-64 firmware. The
target compiles a fixed set of literal entry-point operations; it is not a
general-purpose UEFI runtime.

## Compile the EFI application

Create `uefi_hello.hy`:

```csharp
public class Program {
    public static void Main(string[] args) {
        System.Console.WriteLine("Hello from Hydrogen UEFI!");
    }
}
```

```bash
hy compile uefi_hello.hy --target uefi-x64 -o BOOTX64.EFI
file BOOTX64.EFI
```

`file` should identify a PE32+ EFI application. Place it at
`EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI` on a FAT EFI system partition. It runs under firmware,
not as a Linux executable.

## Firmware console and image launch

The bootloader entry point can clear the console before output, wait for a key
after output, and start one EFI image from the same boot volume:

```csharp
public class Program {
    public static void Main(string[] args) {
        System.Uefi.ClearScreen();
        System.Console.WriteLine("Press a key to boot.");
        System.Uefi.Await();
        System.Uefi.StartImage("\\EFI\\KERNEL.EFI");
    }
}
```

The image path is an ASCII literal. This profile does not support arbitrary
statements or method bodies.

## Early kernel sequence

A separately compiled kernel EFI image can leave boot services and use early
physical-memory, virtual-memory, heap, and framebuffer operations. These calls
must appear in the supported order:

```csharp
public class Program {
    public static void Main(string[] args) {
        System.Console.WriteLine("Starting kernel.");
        System.Uefi.ExitBootServices();
        System.Kernel.MemoryMap.Initialize();
        System.Kernel.Memory.Initialize();
        System.Kernel.VirtualMemory.Initialize();
        System.Kernel.VirtualMemory.ApplyPolicy();
        System.Kernel.Heap.Initialize();
        System.Kernel.Framebuffer.Initialize();
        System.Kernel.Framebuffer.WriteLine("Kernel framebuffer active.");
        System.Kernel.Halt();
    }
}
```

`ExitBootServices()` captures the final UEFI memory map and retries with a fresh
map key when needed. The kernel then reserves conventional memory, installs its
own four-level page tables, and can display printable ASCII through a supported
32-bit RGB or BGR GOP framebuffer. The early heap is a zero-filled, 16-byte
aligned bump allocator; it does not free allocations. Optional
`System.Kernel.Memory.AllocatePage()` calls go after `ApplyPolicy()` and before
`Heap.Initialize()`. Optional `System.Kernel.Heap.Allocate(<literal>)` calls go
after `Heap.Initialize()` and accept literal sizes from 1 byte through 1 MiB.

This path does not provide general object allocation, files, an ordinary
keyboard API, or arbitrary calls after the handoff. A bootloader that calls
`StartImage()` cannot also call `ExitBootServices()`.

## Build an OS project

An `os` manifest can reference separate `efi` and `kernel` projects. Each child
declares an `output` path under the build directory. See
[Projects and references](/tools/projects/) for the manifest format and `hy build`
command. Packaging those images onto boot media is a separate step.

The released compiler supports `public`, `private`, and `protected` members.

| Modifier | Visibility |
| --- | --- |
| `public` | Visible everywhere in the compilation |
| `private` | Restricted to the declaring class |
| `protected` | Available to the declaring class and derived classes |

```csharp
public class Account {
    private int balance;
    public bool isActive;

    public Account(int initial) {
        this.balance = initial;
        this.isActive = true;
    }

    public int GetBalance() { return this.balance; }
}
```

Classes may inherit from a base class. Static methods and fields belong to the
class rather than an instance.

The released compiler supports managed arrays of its supported element types.

```csharp
int[] values = new int[5];
string[] names = new string[3];

values[0] = 42;
int first = values[0];
int length = values.Length;
```

Array counts must be `int`; `void[]` is invalid. Elements are zero/default
initialized. Arrays are mutable reference types, so assignment and passing to a
method retain the same underlying array. Out-of-range access is a runtime error.

Arrays may contain primitive values, strings, enums, structs, and class references:

```csharp
Pair[] pairs = new Pair[2];
pairs[0] = new Pair(9, "pair");
```

The current release has no built-in `List<T>`, map, set, or generic collection.
Use arrays and explicit counters until the standard library provides them.

The released compiler supports `if`, `while`, `for`, `break`, `continue`, and
`return`.

```csharp
int i = 0;
while (i < 100) {
    if (i == 50) {
        break;
    }
    if (i == 25) {
        i = i + 1;
        continue;
    }
    System.Console.WriteLine(i);
    i = i + 1;
}
```

Use `for` when initialization, condition, and update belong together:

```csharp
for (int index = 0; index < 10; index = index + 1) {
    System.Console.WriteLine(index);
}
```

`return;` exits a `void` method. `return expression;` supplies a value from a
non-void method. Exceptions are not part of the current release; use a status
value or explicit condition for recoverable errors.

Enums are declared alongside classes at namespace scope:

```csharp
namespace Demo {
    public enum Direction { North, South, East, West }

    public class Program {
        public static int Main() {
            Direction direction = Direction.North;
            if (direction == Direction.North) {
                System.Console.WriteLine("Heading north");
            }
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```

Members without an explicit value begin at zero and increase by one. Enums can be
fields, locals, parameters, return values, and array elements. Compare members of
the same enum with `==` or `!=`. Console output formats an enum numerically, so
select a string explicitly when human-readable output matters.

Methods belong to classes or structs. The released compiler supports static and
instance methods, constructors, fields, parameters, return values, overloads,
inheritance, interfaces, virtual dispatch, and recursion.

```csharp
namespace Counters {
    public class Counter {
        private int value;

        public Counter(int initial) { value = initial; }

        public int Add(int amount) {
            value = value + amount;
            return value;
        }
    }

    public class Program {
        public static int Double(int value) { return value * 2; }

        public static int Main() {
            Counter counter = new Counter(10);
            System.Console.WriteLine(counter.Add(Double(3)));
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```

`new Counter(10)` allocates a managed reference object and invokes its constructor.
Field initializers run before the constructor body. A class without a declared
constructor has a parameterless default constructor. Fields start at zero, `false`,
or `null` when not initialized.

Class assignment copies a reference, not an object. `this` refers to the current
instance. Static methods have no `this`; pass an object explicitly to access its
instance members.

An overload can use the same name with different parameter types. Derived classes
can call base constructors and use inherited members; interface calls dispatch
to the implementing class or struct. Struct assignment copies the value, while
class assignment copies a reference. Static fields are shared by the class.
Generic methods are not part of this release.

Hydrogen programs contain namespaces and types; executable statements belong inside
methods. There are no top-level statements or free functions.

```csharp
namespace MyApp {
    public class Program {
        public static int Main(string[] args) {
            System.Console.WriteLine("Hello, Hydrogen!");
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```

Namespaces use braces. A source can also declare a class in the global namespace.

## Imports

```csharp
using MyLibrary;
```

Place imports before declarations. They let a file use simple names for types in
that namespace but do not leak to other files. Use fully qualified builtin names,
such as `System.Console.WriteLine`; this is the stable spelling recognized by the
released compiler.

## Entry point

The released compiler accepts static `Main` methods returning `void` or `int`,
with either no parameters or one `string[]` parameter. Project source files are
listed in a `.hyproj` manifest; namespaces do not require a directory layout.
